<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jesus</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 4 BCE – c. 30/33 CE</span> · <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a> · Also known as Jesus of Nazareth, Jesus Christ · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-david">House of David</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jesus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a> was a Jewish preacher from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> whose life and teaching are the basis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a>. He taught in Galilee and the surrounding regions under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herod-antipas">Herod Antipas</a> and was crucified at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> on the orders of the Roman prefect <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontius-pilate">Pontius Pilate</a>, around 30–33 CE. His followers proclaimed his resurrection and spread the new faith across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and beyond.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jesus.jpg" alt="Jesus" width="320" height="622" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown artist · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Spas_vsederzhitel_sinay_(cropped1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>A Jewish person in the time of Jesus usually had only one name, sometimes followed by a patronymic or a home town, and so Jesus is commonly called Jesus of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nazareth">Nazareth</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a>. His name is the English transliteration, through Latin Iesus, of the Greek rendering of the Hebrew Yehoshua, shortened by that period to Yeshua; it means God saves. The word Christ is a title, not a given name, from the Greek Christos, meaning anointed, a calque of the Hebrew mashiakh, or messiah.</p><p>According to Christian tradition as represented in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">Gospels</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-the-apostles">Acts of the Apostles</a>, Jesus was circumcised at eight days old and presented at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a> at 40 days. He was baptised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-the-baptist">John the Baptist</a> as a young adult. After 40 days and nights of fasting in the Judean wilderness he began his public ministry.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The four <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gospels">canonical gospels</a>, Matthew, Mark, Luke and John, are the foremost sources for his life and message. By the usual scholarly view, Mark was written first, and Matthew and Luke used it as a source.</p><p>According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-testament">New Testament</a> accounts, Jesus was an itinerant teacher whom his followers believed to possess divine authority in interpreting Jewish law. He debated with other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, most commonly the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pharisees">Pharisees</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sadducees">Sadducees</a>, engaged in healings, taught in parables, and gathered followers, 12 of whom he appointed as his apostles. He was arrested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, tried by the Sanhedrin, and handed over to the Roman authorities, and was crucified on the order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pontius-pilate">Pontius Pilate</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Christians hold that Jesus rose from the dead on the third day after his death, a belief that became the foundation of the early Christian Church. His first followers were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, and the first Christian community was centred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, with Peter, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-brother-of-jesus">James the brother of Jesus</a>, and John the Apostle among its leaders. After his conversion <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paul-the-apostle">Paul the Apostle</a> spread his teachings to non-Jewish communities in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>. By the end of the 1st century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> began to be recognised as a separate religion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a>.</p><p>Scholars have pursued three quests for the historical Jesus since the 18th century. They agree that he existed, though their portraits differ from each other and from the gospels. Mainstream <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judaism">Judaism</a> rejects the belief that he was the awaited messiah. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> he is considered the messiah and a prophet of God, and most Muslims do not believe he was crucified.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jesus” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bede of Jarrow (c. 673–735) wrote the Ecclesiastical History of the English People, completed in 731, the foundation of English historical writing, and his works on time-reckoning helped spread the practice of dating events from the birth of <b>Christ</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-northumbria">Kingdom of Northumbria · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A later verse held that Liège owed Notger to <b>Christ</b> and everything else to Notger.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Politically, Stephen the Great’s resistance to the Ottomans made him a national hero, and Pope Sixtus IV praised him as an “athlete of <b>Christ</b>”.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-moldavia">Principality of Moldavia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Christianity spread from the 3rd century, and the Hinton St Mary mosaic shows an early image of <b>Christ</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-britain">Roman Britain · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Council of Chalcedon in 451 deposed Patriarch Dioscorus, most Egyptian Christians rejected its definition of <b>Christ</b>'s two natures, and two rival churches emerged: the imperial, Chalcedonian church and the Miaphysite church that became the Coptic Orthodox Church, each with its own patriarch from the 530s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/roman-egypt">Roman Egypt · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Old Saxon epic Heliand, written around 830, retold the life of <b>Christ</b> in Saxon alliterative verse, presenting Christ as a lord and the disciples as his retainers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saxons">Saxons · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bethlehem">Bethlehem</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. Died in Calvary, in what is now <a href="/countries/palestine">Palestine</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=31.7,35.21&amp;year=20" aria-label="Bethlehem on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5457 1009 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Jesus"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5907 1279h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5907 1279h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=31.7,35.21&amp;year=20">What was here in 20 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-family"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Family</h2><ul class="enc-seealso enc-fam"><li><a href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/mary-mother-of-jesus.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="44" height="44"><span><span class="nm">Mary, mother of Jesus</span><span class="tp">Mother &#183; 1st century BCE – 1st century CE</span></span></a></li></ul><p class="ft-note">Parents, spouses and children as Wikidata records them, among the people of the encyclopedia: not the whole family.</p></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Love your enemies, bless them that curse you, do good to them that hate you, and pray for them which despitefully use you, and persecute you…&#8221;</p><footer>Sermon on the Mount, Matthew 5:44 &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as Matthew's Gospel records his teaching &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Matthew" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…why beholdest thou the mote that is in thy brother's eye, but considerest not the beam that is in thine own eye?&#8221;</p><footer>Sermon on the Mount, Matthew 7:3 &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as Matthew's Gospel records his teaching &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Matthew" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;It is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle, than for a rich man to enter into the kingdom of God.&#8221;</p><footer>Matthew 19:24, to his disciples after the rich young man went away &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as Matthew's Gospel records it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Matthew" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Render therefore unto Cesar the things which are Cesar's; and unto God the things that are God's.&#8221;</p><footer>Matthew 22:21, asked whether it was lawful to pay tribute to Caesar &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as Matthew's Gospel records it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Matthew" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;He that is without sin among you, let him first cast a stone at her.&#8221;</p><footer>John 8:7, to those who brought him a woman taken in adultery &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as John's Gospel records it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/John" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Father, forgive them; for they know not what they do.&#8221;</p><footer>Luke 23:34, on the cross &#183; King James Version, 1611 &#183; as Luke's Gospel records it &#183; <a href="https://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Bible_(King_James)/Luke" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
